{#
  Migrations 0050 / 0050a — THE PERMIT HEAD.
  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5 (verbatim), §4.1 laws 1-3 and 9, §16 MI02-MI06;
  spec/TRAPPOINT-SPEC.md R-2, R-3; research/05-architecture/merge-gate-invariant.md §3.2.

  This file is the PIN half of the kernel idiom. It creates nothing that computes and
  nothing that decides; it creates the surface every later mechanism refuses against.

  ── WHY SIX NAMES AND NOT ONE COUNTER ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  `open_blocking = 0` alone would enforce the invariant. Six independently named CHECKs
  cost six times the DDL and buy one sentence: "the merge was refused by
  boundary_certified_when_issued" is a materially better exhibit than "a counter was
  non-zero". Specification rule R-2 makes the collapse non-conformant even where it is
  logically equivalent, and each counter carries an independent trigger, which is what
  makes the refusal-depth matrix mean anything at all. DO NOT CONSOLIDATE THEM.

  ── LAW 1: A CHECK SEES ONLY THE ROW BEING WRITTEN ────────────────────────────────
  No subquery, no join, no now(), no UDF-of-column. That law is the entire reason the
  counters exist as scalar columns of this row: the cross-row fact ("an obligation is
  open somewhere else") is PROJECTED onto the subject by a trigger, and then a plain
  column CHECK refuses over it — for every writer, forever, including a DBA with a psql
  session and including a restored backup being fixed up by hand.

  Every counter column here is written by a projection trigger from an authoritative
  relation the writing role cannot write, NEVER from the inserter (P-2, I02). The
  triggers live in migrations 0100-0109 / 0120-0129 and belong to another worker; this
  file creates the columns they own and the CHECKs that read them. A rendered
  `-- @projects` block below names every one of them so the seam is visible in the
  committed SQL rather than only in a plan.

  ── LAW 2: DEFERRABLE IS UNIMPLEMENTED, SO THE LAST WRITE MUST TRIP ───────────────
  Every intermediate state must be legal at a statement boundary. That is why the
  completing transition writes `state` LAST (spec §5.3 step 7): the CHECKs on this table
  fire on that final write, with the completion record already in place and the epoch
  already pinned.

  ── LAW 9: NO GENERATORS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  `head_seq` and `gate_epoch` are derived by the caller inside the transaction and
  defended by compare-and-swap — `UNIQUE (permit_id, prev_seq)` on the event chain, and
  `UNIQUE (permit_id, gate_epoch)` here as the pin's foreign-key target. A generator that
  allocates outside the transaction would make a gap ambiguous, and the whole evidentiary
  value of this schema is that a gap MEANS tampering.

  ── ONE COLUMN FAMILY, DELIBERATELY ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  Column families are PERMITTED on this table and are NOT used, and the reason is not
  changefeeds — this table is not a changefeed source (§4.1 law 11 confines that to
  `<vertical>_ops.outbox`). The reason is anomaly A1. The gate's correctness argument is
  the MATERIALISED conflict: an obligation write and a merge write touch the same row, so
  the loser gets 40001 rather than a phantom nobody detects. CockroachDB stores each
  column family under its own key, and two writes to two families of one row do not
  collide. Splitting the counters into their own family would therefore un-weld the
  conflict exactly where anomaly A12 needs it most — at READ COMMITTED, where the
  isolation level is no longer doing the work. Conformance case CF-43 asserts 40001 with
  `permit.open_blocking` as the exhibit; that assertion is only true while this table has
  one family.

  ── WHAT THIS TEMPLATE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT EMIT ─────────────────────────────────
  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5 writes `ref_name STRING NOT NULL REFERENCES mainline.ref (ref_name)`.
  That foreign key is NOT emitted here, and the omission is forced by the seam ruling
  MR-1: `permit` is SUBSTRATE (MR-2 names it) and `ref` is VERTICAL (MAINLINE migration
  0026, an authored band). A substrate template that named a vertical relation would not
  apply against the reference binding, where no such relation exists — the file would
  fail at migration time with 42P01, which is outside the refusal taxonomy entirely and
  is the worst possible place to discover a binding error. The column, its NOT NULL and
  its stated-ness CHECK are all here; a vertical that owns a `ref` relation adds
  `ALTER TABLE … ADD CONSTRAINT fk_permit_ref …` in its OWN authored band. The binding
  schema has no key that could express "this vertical owns the repository spine"; adding
  one is a MINOR change to spec/binding/vertical.schema.json and is the right fix.
#}
{% set permit_subjects = subjects | selectattr('kind', 'equalto', 'permit') | list %}
{% if permit_subjects | length != 1 %}
{# The binding decides which subject kinds exist; the kernel decides which migration
   numbers they occupy. 0050 is the permit's number. A binding with no permit subject, or
   with two, has no rendering here, and the refusal below names itself. #}
{{ the_binding_must_declare_exactly_one_subject_of_kind_permit }}
{% endif %}
{% set s = permit_subjects[0] %}
{#
  Three names this template needs that the render context does not yet carry, stated
  here rather than guessed at a call site:

  * `offset_of` duplicates the binding's `[[subject.counters]].offset_column`. The
    context exposes a counter's column, constraint and polarity but not its offset
    column, so an `offset_allowed` counter whose offset is not listed here is a RENDER
    REFUSAL (a KeyError the engine reports against this template) rather than a silently
    weaker CHECK. When the context grows the field, `offset_of[c.column]` becomes
    `c.offset_column` and not one byte of emitted SQL changes.
  * `merge_evidence_constraint` is fixed by the conformance manifest (case CF-04), which
    is the authority for every exhibit name.
  * the two output filenames are §18 / MR-8 anchors and are cited by number elsewhere.
#}
{% set offset_of = {'unmet_floor_count': 'countersigned_count'} %}
{% set merge_evidence_constraint = 'merge_evidence' %}
{% set counter_columns = [] %}
{% for c in s.counters %}
{% set _ = counter_columns.append(c.column) %}
{% if c.polarity == 'offset_allowed' %}
{% set _ = counter_columns.append(offset_of[c.column]) %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
-- @file 0050_permit.sql
{{ header(file='0050_permit.sql',
          title='CREATE TABLE ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.' ~ s.table ~ ' — the protected branch, and the refusals that guard its merge',
          rationale='The permit is a protected branch and this row is its head. Every cross-row obligation is projected onto a scalar column here by a trigger, and a plain-column CHECK over that scalar then refuses the completing transition for every writer, forever — which is the only form of the rule that survives a second writer nobody anticipated. Six independently named CHECKs rather than one counter because the constraint name is the courtroom exhibit (R-2), and UNIQUE (permit_id, gate_epoch) because the epoch pin needs a foreign-key target: without it a precursor arriving after the merge could be attached to an issued permit and SERIALIZABLE would call that history perfectly legal.',
          mi=['MI02', 'MI03', 'MI04', 'MI05', 'MI06'], i=['I02', 'I03']) }}
--
-- ── THE PROJECTED CROSS-ROW FACTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Each column named below is a MATERIALISED CONFLICT, not a cache. It is written by a
-- projection trigger from a declared authority relation and never by the inserter; a
-- value a client supplies for one of these columns does not survive the write (I02).
-- Absence of the authority row REFUSES — it does not default, infer, or admit-and-flag.
--
{% for column in counter_columns %}
-- @projects   {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }}.{{ column }}
{% endfor %}
-- @projects   {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }}.site_role
-- @on_missing raise
-- @maintained-by the projection band (functions 0100-0109, triggers 0120-0129)
--
-- These are rendered SQL comments and NOT template pragmas, and the difference is
-- deliberate. The Authority Source Contract (rule A-1) requires an [[authority_source]]
-- entry — relation, key, positional columns — behind every pragma'd column. A counter has
-- no such shape: it is an AGGREGATE maintained incrementally from an obligation relation,
-- which the binding declares under [[obligation_source]], not a keyed lookup of one row.
-- Writing the pragma anyway would make `trappoint render` refuse this template for the
-- wrong reason, and would assert in a machine-readable way that a counter is a keyed
-- projection, which it is not. The comment says what is true; the pragma would not.
--
-- ── COLUMN NOTES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- site_role   denormalised RLS scope token, trigger-filled. §4.1 law 10: an RLS policy
--             expression cannot contain a subquery, so the scope must already be a
--             comparable scalar on the row. NAME rather than STRING because the policy
--             compares it against CURRENT_USER.
-- head_seq    the event chain's head. Derived in-transaction and defended by the CAS on
--             the chain, never by a generator that commits outside the transaction.
-- gate_epoch  increments iff the gate OPENS. The pin's foreign-key target is
--             (permit_id, gate_epoch), so once a completion record exists this column is
--             physically immutable and a new obligation therefore cannot be attached.
-- under_hold  an operational stop that is NOT a gate condition. It is recorded so the
--             console can show it; nothing in this file reads it, on purpose. A hold that
--             silently blocked the merge would be a second, undocumented gate.
-- horizon_at  the permit's own bound. `>=` rather than `>` because opened_at defaults to
--             now() and a caller supplying now() for the horizon in the same statement
--             gets the identical transaction timestamp — a strict comparison would refuse
--             a correct write for a reason that has nothing to do with safety.
--
-- ── NO ON DELETE CASCADE, ANYWHERE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
-- The parent link is RESTRICT in both directions. A cascade rewrites history, which is
-- the precise offence this substrate exists to detect. `permit_parent_not_self` is the
-- other half: the declared remedy for a post-completion fact is a FORK — suspend the
-- issued permit and open a CHILD whose gate is cleared afresh — and a permit that is its
-- own parent is a fork that forked nothing.

CREATE TABLE {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} (
  {{ '%-22s'|format(s.id_column) }} UUID NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  {{ '%-22s'|format('site_id') }} UUID NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('site_role') }} NAME NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('external_ref') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('ref_name') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('parent_' ~ s.id_column) }} UUID NULL,
  {{ '%-22s'|format(s.state_column) }} {{ binding.schema }}.subject_state NOT NULL DEFAULT 'draft',
  {{ '%-22s'|format('head_seq') }} INT8 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  {{ '%-22s'|format(s.epoch_column) }} INT8 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('merged_commit') }} BYTES NULL,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('under_hold') }} BOOL NOT NULL DEFAULT false,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('slice_digest') }} BYTES NULL,
  {{ '%-22s'|format('opened_at') }} TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  {{ '%-22s'|format('horizon_at') }} TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,

  -- ▼▼ PROJECTED CROSS-ROW FACTS. Trigger-written, never supplied. ▼▼
{% for column in counter_columns %}
  {{ '%-22s'|format(column) }} INT8 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
{% endfor %}
  -- ▲▲

  CONSTRAINT ctr_nonneg CHECK (
{% for column in counter_columns %}
    {{ column }} >= 0{{ ' AND' if not loop.last }}
{% endfor %}
  ),

  -- ▼▼ THE PRODUCT. {{ (s.counters | length) + 1 }} independent refusals; each name is the exhibit. ▼▼
{% for c in s.counters %}
{% if c.polarity == 'offset_allowed' %}
  CONSTRAINT {{ c.constraint }}
      CHECK ({{ s.state_column }} <> '{{ s.completing_state }}'
             OR {{ c.column }} = 0
             OR {{ offset_of[c.column] }} > 0),
{% else %}
  CONSTRAINT {{ '%-30s'|format(c.constraint) }} CHECK ({{ s.state_column }} <> '{{ s.completing_state }}' OR {{ c.column }} = 0),
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
  CONSTRAINT {{ '%-30s'|format(merge_evidence_constraint) }} CHECK ({{ s.state_column }} <> '{{ s.completing_state }}' OR merged_commit IS NOT NULL),
  -- ▲▲

  CONSTRAINT permit_ledger_nonneg       CHECK (head_seq >= 0 AND {{ s.epoch_column }} >= 0),
  CONSTRAINT permit_external_ref_stated CHECK (external_ref <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT permit_ref_name_stated     CHECK (ref_name <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT permit_site_role_stated    CHECK (site_role <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT permit_horizon_bounded     CHECK (horizon_at >= opened_at),
  CONSTRAINT permit_commit_sized        CHECK (merged_commit IS NULL OR length(merged_commit) = 32),
  CONSTRAINT permit_slice_digest_sized  CHECK (slice_digest IS NULL OR length(slice_digest) = 32),
  CONSTRAINT permit_parent_not_self
    CHECK (parent_{{ s.id_column }} IS NULL OR parent_{{ s.id_column }} <> {{ s.id_column }}),
  CONSTRAINT fk_parent_permit FOREIGN KEY (parent_{{ s.id_column }})
    REFERENCES {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} ({{ s.id_column }})
    ON UPDATE RESTRICT ON DELETE RESTRICT,
  CONSTRAINT pk_permit PRIMARY KEY ({{ s.id_column }}),
  -- The epoch pin's foreign-key target. Without this UNIQUE the pin cannot be declared,
  -- and without the pin a precursor arriving after the merge is a perfectly serializable
  -- history that quietly reopens an issued permit (anomaly A2).
  CONSTRAINT permit_epoch_target        UNIQUE ({{ s.id_column }}, {{ s.epoch_column }}),
  CONSTRAINT permit_external_ref_unique UNIQUE (site_id, external_ref)
);
-- @file 0050a_permit_scope_index.sql
{{ header(file='0050a_permit_scope_index.sql',
          title='CREATE INDEX permit_scope — the RLS scope token first, then state, then recency',
          rationale='Leading on site_role rather than site_id is what makes the row-level security predicate an index seek instead of a filter over every permit in the cluster. Law 10 forbids a subquery in a policy expression, so the scope MUST already be a scalar on the row; this index is the half of that decision that makes it affordable. state second and opened_at DESC third serve the one query the console and the audit summary both run — the open gate for a scope, newest first.',
          mi=['MI02'], i=['I02']) }}
--
-- Declared as its own migration rather than inline, because one statement per file is
-- not negotiable: CockroachDB DDL is not transactional across statements, so an index
-- build that fails beside a CREATE TABLE leaves a version nobody can diagnose.
--
-- NOT partial. A permit in every state is in scope for the audit views, and a partial
-- index over the open states would silently stop serving the closed-permit history an
-- investigation reads first.

CREATE INDEX permit_scope
  ON {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} (site_role, {{ s.state_column }}, opened_at DESC);
